  "raw_text": "Step 1: Expand the right side of the given equation to match the form of the left side.\n\nStep 2: The given equation is \\(f(x) - f(a) = (x - a)^2 (x - b)\\). Expanding the right side, we get:\n\\[\n(x - a)^2 (x - b) = (x^2 - 2ax + a^2)(x - b) = x^3 - bx^2 - 2ax^2 + 2abx + a^2x - a^2b = x^3 - (2a+b)x^2 + (2ab+a^2)x - a^2b.\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Since \\(f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 1\\), then \\(f(a) = a^3 + 3a^2 + 1\\). Therefore, the left side of the equation becomes:\n\\[\nf(x) - f(a) = (x^3 + 3x^2 + 1) - (a^3 + 3a^2 + 1) = x^3 + 3x^2 - a^3 - 3a^2.\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Equate the coefficients from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nx^3 + 3x^2 - a^3 - 3a^2 = x^3 - (2a+b)x^2 + (2ab+a^2)x - a^2b.\n\\]\n\nStep 5: From the coefficient of \\(x^2\\), we have:\n\\[\n3 = -(2a + b).\n\\]\nFrom the constant term, we have:\n\\[\n-a^3 - 3a^2 = -a^2b \\implies a^3 + 3a^2 = a^2b \\implies a^2(a + 3) = a^2b \\implies a + 3 = b \\quad (\\text{since } a \\neq 0).\n\\]\n\nStep 6: Substitute \\(b = a + 3\\) into the equation for the coefficient of \\(x^2\\):\n\\[\n3 = -(2a + (a + 3)) \\implies 3 = -(3a + 3) \\implies 3 = -3a - 3 \\implies 6 = -3a \\implies a = -2.\n\\]\n\nStep 7: Using \\(a = -2\\), find \\(b\\):\n\\[\nb = a + 3 = -2 + 3 = 1.\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{(-2, 1)}<|im_end|>",
